1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,460
Hi everyone, and welcome to the Digital Factory Practicum at WenQuest.

2
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:11,282
This is Lecture 10, business. Margin, receivables and cash.

3
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:14,670
Making money is not the same as having money.

4
00:00:16,190 --> 00:00:18,290
Here's what happened.

5
00:00:18,690 --> 00:00:26,329
The 20 joint reducers the robot plant added have all been delivered and

6
00:00:26,349 --> 00:00:31,075
invoiced for 33,000 dollars on 30-day terms.

7
00:00:31,475 --> 00:00:35,855
It's now 15 days past due and the money hasn't come.

8
00:00:36,255 --> 00:00:42,721
There's 60,000 in the bank and 8,000 a week of fixed costs: wages,

9
00:00:42,741 --> 00:00:44,195
rent and so on.

10
00:00:44,595 --> 00:00:50,918
Sales says the robot plant is a big customer and chasing will hurt the relationship;

11
00:00:50,938 --> 00:00:56,052
accounts says if we don't collect, in two months we can't pay wages.

12
00:00:56,452 --> 00:00:56,922
Who's right?

13
00:00:58,442 --> 00:01:00,942
How much did the order make.

14
00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:04,628
Cost comes in three blocks.

15
00:01:05,028 --> 00:01:15,050
Material: each part's BOM quantity times its price, 538.74 a unit.

16
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:21,098
Labour: each operation's time times the workstation rate,

17
00:01:21,118 --> 00:01:27,262
which holds depreciation and wages; the grinder, for instance,

18
00:01:27,282 --> 00:01:32,630
is 20 dollars of depreciation and 26 of wages an hour.

19
00:01:33,030 --> 00:01:39,330
All operations together give 571.80 a unit.

20
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:46,950
The digital factory books depreciation with wages as labour, a conversion cost;

21
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:51,991
accountants put depreciation in manufacturing overhead.

22
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:59,970
Energy, the energy part of the rates, is 73.87.

23
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:10,630
Standard cost 1,184.41; sold at 1,650, a standard margin of 28.2 percent.

24
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:18,074
And margin isn't profit: it still has to pay overheads, selling costs,

25
00:02:18,094 --> 00:02:19,789
interest and tax.

26
00:02:21,310 --> 00:02:25,770
What you actually spend is often more than standard.

27
00:02:26,170 --> 00:02:32,593
The digital factory splits an order's money into seven lines: material, labour,

28
00:02:32,613 --> 00:02:38,302
energy, scrap and rework, idle power, rush and overtime, and warranty,

29
00:02:38,322 --> 00:02:40,667
each traceable to its source.

30
00:02:41,067 --> 00:02:51,428
This order's 33,000 of revenue, cut block by block, leaves a margin of 8,413,

31
00:02:51,448 --> 00:02:53,585
or 25.5 percent.

32
00:02:53,985 --> 00:03:01,758
The dashed line is the margin at standard cost, 28.2 percent: 2.7 points lost,

33
00:03:01,778 --> 00:03:05,055
just above the 25 percent target.

34
00:03:06,575 --> 00:03:11,567
The unplanned money totals 899 dollars.

35
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:18,193
The largest is overtime: the skilled grinder operator was away,

36
00:03:18,213 --> 00:03:22,853
12 hours of cover at 30 dollars times 1.5, 540.

37
00:03:23,253 --> 00:03:33,622
Re-zeroing and a new wheel after the grinder stopped: 184; two scrapped shafts:

38
00:03:33,642 --> 00:03:42,300
106; bearings 20 percent dearer from the alternative supplier: 64.

39
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:51,752
On screen overtime goes from 12 to 24 and 48 hours and the margin bar shrinks;

40
00:03:51,772 --> 00:03:56,754
then a 5 percent price cut takes even more.

41
00:03:57,154 --> 00:04:05,610
The band saw idling for 24 hours cost only 4.80: judge money by the numbers,

42
00:04:05,630 --> 00:04:07,729
not by impressions.

43
00:04:09,249 --> 00:04:16,917
The big blocks are labour, 35.2 percent of revenue, and material, 32.7;

44
00:04:16,937 --> 00:04:22,114
apart from material, labour is the largest line.

45
00:04:22,514 --> 00:04:26,094
To earn more, look at hours and rates first.

46
00:04:26,494 --> 00:04:32,756
If a margin falls more than 5 points below target, below 20 percent,

47
00:04:32,776 --> 00:04:36,914
the hub flags it and lists the biggest lines.

48
00:04:37,314 --> 00:04:38,014
Who fixes it?

49
00:04:38,414 --> 00:04:43,140
A low price goes to sales, dear material to purchasing,

50
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:47,887
excess hours to process engineering and the shop floor.

51
00:04:48,287 --> 00:04:49,462
By the way,

52
00:04:49,482 --> 00:04:56,634
the 215-dollar after-shift service from Lecture 6 is booked to the

53
00:04:56,654 --> 00:04:58,698
maintenance budget,

54
00:04:58,718 --> 00:05:00,546
not to the order.

55
00:05:02,066 --> 00:05:04,406
When does the money come back.

56
00:05:05,926 --> 00:05:09,578
Delivered, invoiced, not paid: that's a receivable,

57
00:05:09,598 --> 00:05:13,106
the plant's money still in the customer's pocket.

58
00:05:13,506 --> 00:05:17,486
First the ageing, sorted by days overdue.

59
00:05:17,886 --> 00:05:23,861
Receivables are 62,700, 63 percent overdue.

60
00:05:24,261 --> 00:05:32,050
On screen the robot plant's invoice goes from 0 to 15, 35 and 70 days overdue,

61
00:05:32,070 --> 00:05:38,457
dropping band by band; the older it gets, the harder to collect.

62
00:05:38,857 --> 00:05:44,413
Then credit: it owes 33,000, already over its 30,000 limit,

63
00:05:44,433 --> 00:05:50,462
and counting its next batch, in production and not yet invoiced,

64
00:05:50,482 --> 00:05:57,077
exposure is already 165 percent, so the hub raises a second exception:

65
00:05:57,097 --> 00:05:58,779
over credit limit.

66
00:06:00,299 --> 00:06:04,439
Whether receivables are high depends on sales.

67
00:06:04,839 --> 00:06:07,455
DSO, days sales outstanding,

68
00:06:07,475 --> 00:06:12,539
says how many days of sales the receivables represent.

69
00:06:12,939 --> 00:06:22,950
Only two invoices went out in the last 30 days, GreenValley and Harbor Crane,

70
00:06:22,970 --> 00:06:25,686
23,100, or 770 a day.

71
00:06:26,086 --> 00:06:35,866
DSO is 62,700 over 770: 81.4 days, against a target of 45.

72
00:06:36,266 --> 00:06:41,886
Once the robot plant pays, it falls to 38.6.

73
00:06:42,286 --> 00:06:50,706
DSO converts to money: every day off brings 770 dollars back to the bank.

74
00:06:52,226 --> 00:06:54,997
Margin answers whether we make money;

75
00:06:55,017 --> 00:06:58,166
cash answers whether we can pay the bills.

76
00:06:58,566 --> 00:07:04,146
Wages, rent and suppliers are paid in cash, not in receivables.

77
00:07:04,546 --> 00:07:08,888
The digital factory forecasts 8 weeks ahead:

78
00:07:08,908 --> 00:07:14,935
this week's cash is last week's plus cash in, minus cash out,

79
00:07:14,955 --> 00:07:16,719
minus fixed costs.

80
00:07:17,119 --> 00:07:22,571
Receipts come on the due date plus the customer's usual delay:

81
00:07:22,591 --> 00:07:28,219
Summit in week 1, GreenValley in week 4, Harbor Crane in week 5.

82
00:07:28,619 --> 00:07:34,374
Three material bills go out in weeks 3, 5 and 7.

83
00:07:34,774 --> 00:07:41,234
Cash must stay above zero every week, ideally with a week of costs to spare.

84
00:07:42,754 --> 00:07:44,671
First, doing nothing.

85
00:07:45,071 --> 00:07:49,411
The robot plant won't pay unless chased,

86
00:07:49,431 --> 00:07:53,771
so its 33,000 doesn't arrive in 8 weeks.

87
00:07:54,171 --> 00:08:00,276
On screen the cash is drawn week by week: cash in as green bars,

88
00:08:00,296 --> 00:08:01,807
cash out as red.

89
00:08:02,207 --> 00:08:09,567
In week 7 only 1,376 is left, below the safety line;

90
00:08:09,587 --> 00:08:16,947
in week 8 it's minus 6,624: the wages can't be paid.

91
00:08:18,467 --> 00:08:29,923
The order clearly made 8,413, yet week 8 shows minus 6,624.

92
00:08:30,323 --> 00:08:34,700
That's how a profitable factory goes bankrupt:

93
00:08:34,720 --> 00:08:38,523
materials and wages were paid out first,

94
00:08:38,543 --> 00:08:43,398
while 33,000 of sales money sits with the customer.

95
00:08:43,798 --> 00:08:48,374
The longer the money is on the road, the more cash you advance;

96
00:08:48,394 --> 00:08:52,898
the more orders and the faster you grow, the more you advance.

97
00:08:53,298 --> 00:08:58,758
Many plants don't die of losses; they die when the cash runs out.

98
00:09:00,278 --> 00:09:02,898
This is Virtual lab 10-2.

99
00:09:03,298 --> 00:09:07,598
The robot plant's payment day slides from day 3 onward.

100
00:09:07,998 --> 00:09:09,812
Paid on day 3,

101
00:09:09,832 --> 00:09:18,850
the whole curve lifts and the lowest cash in 8 weeks is still 26,376.

102
00:09:19,250 --> 00:09:20,687
Paid on day 50,

103
00:09:20,707 --> 00:09:26,904
the first 7 weeks match doing nothing and week 7 has only 1,376,

104
00:09:26,924 --> 00:09:32,344
below the safety line; any later and it goes below zero.

105
00:09:32,744 --> 00:09:36,524
The red dashed line is the do-nothing control.

106
00:09:36,924 --> 00:09:42,482
Each week the customer is late, part of the curve sinks.

107
00:09:44,002 --> 00:09:46,902
The hub offers three options.

108
00:09:47,302 --> 00:09:52,362
A, do nothing: cash goes negative in week 8.

109
00:09:52,762 --> 00:09:59,037
B, a dunning letter: customers usually pay within 3 days,

110
00:09:59,057 --> 00:10:07,209
the lowest cash in 8 weeks is 26,376, and the relationship stays friendly.

111
00:10:07,609 --> 00:10:12,204
C, a credit freeze: dun and stop new shipments,

112
00:10:12,224 --> 00:10:17,800
so the next 16,500 batch is neither shipped nor invoiced.

113
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:21,935
For the overdue invoice, C is the same as B;

114
00:10:21,954 --> 00:10:26,543
C also delays the next batch's invoice and collection,

115
00:10:26,563 --> 00:10:32,260
which the 8-week forecast leaves out because it isn't invoiced yet.

116
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:40,303
C adds damage control, at the price of the relationship.

117
00:10:40,703 --> 00:10:42,638
The hub has two rules:

118
00:10:42,658 --> 00:10:48,325
the overdue rule looks only at days and recommends B at 15 days;

119
00:10:48,345 --> 00:10:54,723
the credit-limit rule looks at exposure and recommends C at 165 percent.

120
00:10:55,123 --> 00:10:58,070
When they clash, a person weighs them:

121
00:10:58,090 --> 00:11:04,239
we dun first and freeze if it's still unpaid before the next batch is invoiced.

122
00:11:05,759 --> 00:11:07,823
Doing nothing isn't free;

123
00:11:07,843 --> 00:11:12,657
its price isn't in the margin table but in the cash table:

124
00:11:12,677 --> 00:11:18,325
borrow and pay interest, or delay suppliers and hurt your scorecard.

125
00:11:18,725 --> 00:11:21,843
Still, sometimes it's better not to chase:

126
00:11:21,863 --> 00:11:26,700
a reliable customer with plenty of orders in a short rough patch,

127
00:11:26,720 --> 00:11:30,585
or a small sum where chasing could lose a big order.

128
00:11:30,985 --> 00:11:34,308
Then phone, find out what's going on,

129
00:11:34,328 --> 00:11:38,645
agree a payment date and put it in the forecast.

130
00:11:39,045 --> 00:11:41,465
Weigh money and the relationship together.

131
00:11:42,985 --> 00:11:45,684
In the digital factory.

132
00:11:47,204 --> 00:11:54,686
After class, do Lab 22 on the digital factory's Business and finance page.

133
00:11:55,086 --> 00:11:59,231
First find the missed objectives in the objective tree;

134
00:11:59,251 --> 00:12:04,909
then open the order your instructor names and calculate its margin from the

135
00:12:04,929 --> 00:12:05,666
breakdown.

136
00:12:06,066 --> 00:12:11,956
The instructor injects a 13,200-dollar Harbor Crane invoice,

137
00:12:11,976 --> 00:12:15,995
15 days overdue and unpaid unless chased.

138
00:12:16,395 --> 00:12:17,626
Compare dunning,

139
00:12:17,646 --> 00:12:22,789
a freeze and doing nothing in the command centre and give reasons;

140
00:12:22,809 --> 00:12:26,465
the hub's advice stays hidden until you submit.

141
00:12:26,865 --> 00:12:30,621
Then raise the dunning, the instructor approves,

142
00:12:30,641 --> 00:12:33,925
and a few minutes later the customer pays.

143
00:12:34,325 --> 00:12:38,539
Finally compute DSO and find the tightest week.

144
00:12:38,939 --> 00:12:43,809
In teaching mode a payment day lasts two minutes,

145
00:12:43,829 --> 00:12:50,097
so you see invoices fall overdue and get paid within one class.

146
00:12:51,617 --> 00:12:55,467
Why does the robot plant pay late too?

147
00:12:55,867 --> 00:13:03,786
It sells collaborative robots to integrators, who build cells for end users,

148
00:13:03,806 --> 00:13:06,607
usually paid by milestones.

149
00:13:07,007 --> 00:13:14,076
Take a 120,000-dollar cell, an illustrative figure: 30 percent on signing,

150
00:13:14,096 --> 00:13:21,165
60 percent after site acceptance, and a 10 percent retention a year later.

151
00:13:21,565 --> 00:13:27,280
If acceptance slips two months, 72,000 arrives two months late;

152
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:34,107
the integrator pays the robot plant late, and the robot plant pays us late.

153
00:13:34,507 --> 00:13:39,616
So the robot plant's credit limit should reflect how its own customers pay.

154
00:13:40,016 --> 00:13:47,312
We can also negotiate a down payment, or deliver and invoice in batches.

155
00:13:48,832 --> 00:13:53,977
An everyday example: a shop that lets regulars buy on credit.

156
00:13:54,377 --> 00:14:02,372
It sells 3,000 a month at a 30 percent margin with 600 of rent and wages:

157
00:14:02,392 --> 00:14:04,677
300 a month on paper.

158
00:14:05,077 --> 00:14:12,392
But half goes on credit, repaid two months later, while stock is paid in cash,

159
00:14:12,412 --> 00:14:15,777
and the shop opened with only 1,000.

160
00:14:16,177 --> 00:14:23,186
Cash is negative in month 1 and 1,400 short in month 2:

161
00:14:23,206 --> 00:14:26,637
borrow or run out of stock.

162
00:14:27,037 --> 00:14:29,643
The same problem as today's plant.

163
00:14:31,163 --> 00:14:32,362
Back to the start.

164
00:14:32,762 --> 00:14:37,952
The order made 8,413, 25.5 percent, just on target;

165
00:14:37,972 --> 00:14:43,060
it fell short of standard mainly through overtime,

166
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:46,022
grinder re-setting and scrap.

167
00:14:46,422 --> 00:14:51,482
DSO is 81.4 days, far above target.

168
00:14:51,882 --> 00:14:58,182
Doing nothing, cash reaches minus 6,624 in week 8.

169
00:14:58,582 --> 00:15:07,025
Send the dunning letter now: the money returns in about 3 days,

170
00:15:07,045 --> 00:15:15,086
the lowest cash in 8 weeks is 26,376, and DSO falls to 38.6.

171
00:15:15,486 --> 00:15:20,768
Only 15 days overdue, so the overdue rule says dun;

172
00:15:20,788 --> 00:15:26,798
at 165 percent exposure the credit-limit rule says freeze.

173
00:15:27,198 --> 00:15:33,679
Weighing them, dun first; if it's still unpaid when the next batch is invoiced,

174
00:15:33,699 --> 00:15:37,712
or past 30 days, ask the plant manager to freeze.

175
00:15:38,112 --> 00:15:43,406
Next time, negotiate a down payment or batch invoicing,

176
00:15:43,426 --> 00:15:48,721
and price overtime and dearer suppliers into the quote.

177
00:15:50,241 --> 00:15:57,087
Now Virtual lab 10-1. On the WenQuest platform you can do it yourself.

178
00:15:57,107 --> 00:16:04,541
The numbers in the results panel are blurred here; reading them is your job.

179
00:16:04,941 --> 00:16:11,744
First press robot plant order and read the margin and its percentage.

180
00:16:11,764 --> 00:16:15,995
Check them line by line against Table 10-2.

181
00:16:16,395 --> 00:16:21,735
Then press standard cost and see how many points the unplanned money took off.

182
00:16:22,135 --> 00:16:25,390
Back on the robot plant order,

183
00:16:25,410 --> 00:16:32,485
drag overtime from 0 to 48 hours and watch the margin bar shrink.

184
00:16:32,885 --> 00:16:40,362
Then drag the band saw's idling from 0 to 72 hours: the margin hardly moves.

185
00:16:40,382 --> 00:16:43,025
Judge money by the numbers.

186
00:16:43,425 --> 00:16:49,431
Finally press Play and watch the revenue being cut block by block,

187
00:16:49,451 --> 00:16:51,165
leaving the margin.

188
00:16:52,685 --> 00:16:56,591
Virtual lab 10-2: cash over the next 8 weeks.

189
00:16:56,611 --> 00:17:00,256
Press do nothing and find the lowest week,

190
00:17:00,276 --> 00:17:05,665
and the weeks cash falls below the safety line and below zero.

191
00:17:06,065 --> 00:17:09,992
Then press dun: the whole curve lifts.

192
00:17:10,392 --> 00:17:14,711
Press freeze credit: cash is the same as dunning.

193
00:17:14,731 --> 00:17:18,252
Read the note below to see what differs.

194
00:17:18,652 --> 00:17:25,556
Drag the payment day from day 3 all the way to day 60 and watch the curve

195
00:17:25,576 --> 00:17:27,832
sink section by section.

196
00:17:28,232 --> 00:17:34,155
Drag the days overdue past 30 and watch the ageing band and the hub's

197
00:17:34,175 --> 00:17:36,049
recommendation change.

198
00:17:36,449 --> 00:17:40,229
Finally press Play to draw the cash week by week.

199
00:17:40,249 --> 00:17:44,339
I won't give the answers here; they're yours to find.

200
00:17:44,359 --> 00:17:49,457
Both labs come with a guide and a report template on the platform.

201
00:17:50,977 --> 00:17:55,371
After class you'll do Lab 22 in the WenQuest digital factory.

202
00:17:55,391 --> 00:17:59,352
First find the missed objectives in the objective tree,

203
00:17:59,372 --> 00:18:04,779
then open the order your instructor names and calculate its margin from the

204
00:18:04,799 --> 00:18:05,503
breakdown.

205
00:18:05,903 --> 00:18:11,817
The instructor injects an unpaid overdue invoice. Compare dunning,

206
00:18:11,837 --> 00:18:16,851
a credit freeze and doing nothing in the command centre,

207
00:18:16,871 --> 00:18:20,987
choose one and give reasons. Until you submit,

208
00:18:21,007 --> 00:18:25,123
the factory hub's recommendation stays hidden.

209
00:18:25,523 --> 00:18:30,024
Then raise the dunning, the instructor approves it,

210
00:18:30,044 --> 00:18:36,141
and a few minutes later the customer pays and the invoice turns paid.

211
00:18:36,161 --> 00:18:40,307
Finally compute DSO and find the tightest week.

212
00:18:41,827 --> 00:18:45,673
The assignment has three tasks. One, calculation:

213
00:18:45,693 --> 00:18:49,539
if the robot plant demands a 5 percent price cut,

214
00:18:49,559 --> 00:18:55,219
does the margin still meet target? If overtime goes from 12 to 24 hours,

215
00:18:55,239 --> 00:19:00,663
what's the margin, and what's the lowest price that holds 25 percent?

216
00:19:01,063 --> 00:19:03,381
Two, cash: compute a DSO;

217
00:19:03,401 --> 00:19:09,179
then suppose the robot plant pays unchased but only on day 50,

218
00:19:09,199 --> 00:19:14,043
forecast 8 weeks of cash and find the tightest week.

219
00:19:14,443 --> 00:19:20,502
Three, the Lab 22 report, focusing on how you compared the options,

220
00:19:20,522 --> 00:19:25,583
and answering: why can a profitable factory go bankrupt?

221
00:19:27,103 --> 00:19:31,831
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example:

222
00:19:31,851 --> 00:19:39,881
receivables of 45,000 and 27,000 invoiced in the last 30 days. What's the DSO?

223
00:19:40,281 --> 00:19:47,024
If an order's margin meets the target, can the plant still run out of cash?

224
00:19:47,424 --> 00:19:50,067
You can take the quiz three times,

225
00:19:50,087 --> 00:19:53,984
and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

226
00:19:55,504 --> 00:19:57,644
Three things to remember.

227
00:19:58,044 --> 00:20:03,654
One: break down the margin, revenue minus material, labour,

228
00:20:03,674 --> 00:20:11,002
energy and unplanned costs, to see where the money went; price goes to sales,

229
00:20:11,022 --> 00:20:16,060
material to purchasing, hours to process engineering.

230
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:23,067
Two: read receivables by ageing and DSO, forecast cash week by week,

231
00:20:23,087 --> 00:20:25,600
and watch the lowest week.

232
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:32,904
Three: an overdue invoice is judged by days, dunning first within 30;

233
00:20:32,924 --> 00:20:36,617
an over-limit customer gets a freeze,

234
00:20:36,637 --> 00:20:41,334
and when the two disagree a person weighs them;

235
00:20:41,354 --> 00:20:44,645
making money is not having money.

236
00:20:45,045 --> 00:20:51,185
After class: Assignment 10, Quiz 10 and the Lab 22 report.

237
00:20:51,585 --> 00:20:55,351
The virtual labs, the digital-factory lab,

